---
title: Links
order: 4
description: Symbolic and hard links — ln, readlink
tags: [files, ln, symlink, hardlink, readlink, links]
---

Links create additional references to files. Symbolic (soft) links are pointers to a path. Hard links are additional directory entries pointing to the same inode.

@text
Symbolic links vs hard links:

  Symbolic (soft) link:
  - Points to a pathname, not the data
  - Can cross filesystem boundaries
  - Can link to directories
  - Breaks if target is moved or deleted (dangling link)
  - Has its own inode and permissions (target permissions apply)

  Hard link:
  - Points directly to the same inode (same data on disk)
  - Cannot cross filesystem boundaries
  - Cannot link to directories (except . and ..)
  - Original and link are indistinguishable; data persists until all links removed
  - Shares inode, permissions, ownership, and timestamps

@text
Create and manage symbolic links.

@code bash
# Create a symbolic link
ln -s /path/to/target linkname

# Create symlink in current directory with same name as target
ln -s /path/to/target .

# Overwrite existing link
ln -sf /path/to/new-target linkname

# Create relative symlink
ln -sr target linkname

# Create symlink to a directory
ln -s /var/log /home/user/logs

# Verify a symlink
ls -l linkname              # shows linkname -> target

# Read where a symlink points
readlink linkname

# Resolve full canonical path (follow all symlinks)
readlink -f linkname

# Resolve path but don't require last component to exist
readlink -m /path/that/may/not/exist

# realpath — similar to readlink -f
realpath linkname

# Find all symlinks in a directory
find /etc -type l

# Find broken (dangling) symlinks
find /path -xtype l

# Find all symlinks pointing to a specific target
find / -lname "/path/to/target" 2>/dev/null

@text
Create and manage hard links.

@code bash
# Create a hard link
ln target hardlink

# Verify hard link (same inode number)
ls -li target hardlink
stat target hardlink

# Find all hard links to a file (by inode number)
INODE=$(stat -c %i target)
find / -xdev -inum "$INODE" 2>/dev/null

# Check link count for a file
stat -c '%h' filename

@text
Practical examples.

@code bash
# Symlink dotfiles to a repo
ln -sf ~/dotfiles/.bashrc ~/.bashrc
ln -sf ~/dotfiles/.vimrc ~/.vimrc

# Symlink a config directory
ln -s ~/dotfiles/nvim ~/.config/nvim

# Alternative binary version (e.g., python)
sudo ln -sf /usr/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/python

# Backup rotation with hard links (space-efficient)
# Hard-link unchanged files from previous backup
cp -al /backup/previous/ /backup/current/
rsync -a --delete /source/ /backup/current/
